{
  "summary": "This pull request wires NAT-PMP port mapping and Tor control port connections into CConnman's network-active lifecycle. When networking is disabled via `-networkactive=0` or the `setnetworkactive` RPC, NAT-PMP mapping stops and the Tor controller idles without attempting connection or reconnection. Auxiliary network lifecycle updates and subsystem callbacks are serialized under a mutex so state changes cannot race across RPC and GUI calls.",
  "problem": "When `-networkactive=0` is set or networking is toggled off, bitcoind continues performing local network activity: NAT-PMP continues querying local gateways, and the Tor controller repeatedly attempts to connect or reconnect to the Tor control port in the background, violating user expectations of a completely disabled network state.",
  "evidence": [
    "Fixes linked issue #34190 ('bitcoind shouldn't perform network activity if the option networkactive=0')",
    "Author description: 'When networkactive=0 is set, NAT-PMP port mapping and Tor control connections still run in the background, mapping ports and logging retry attempts despite the node being \"inactive.\"'"
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [],
    "enables": []
  },
  "scope_notes": "",
  "changed_since_previous": "Added commit 799116c005 serializing auxiliary network lifecycle callbacks under `m_network_active_mutex` to protect against concurrent RPC and GUI preference toggles, handled reconnect backoff resetting during rapid off/on cycles while sleeping, and added unit and functional tests covering these edge cases.",
  "card": "PR #34486 wires the lifecycle of NAT-PMP port mapping and Tor control connections into CConnman's network-active state so that both pause when networking is disabled and resume upon reactivation. It fixes issue #34190, where bitcoind continued background gateway queries and Tor control reconnect attempts despite running with -networkactive=0. Reconnection backoff is reset on reactivation, and concurrent state changes between RPC and GUI are serialized to avoid desynchronization.",
